The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
-putname(name);
...
if (retry_estale(error, lookup_flags)) {
    lookup_flags |= LOOKUP_REVAL;
    goto retry;
}
```
Reason:
The `putname(name);` function is called before the retry logic, which means that if the operation needs to be retried (due to an error), the `name` object has already been freed. This leads to a use-after-free situation where the code may attempt to access the `name` object again, resulting in undefined behavior.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
+putname(name);
...
if (retry_estale(error, lookup_flags)) {
    lookup_flags |= LOOKUP_REVAL;
    goto retry;
}
```
Reason:
The `putname(name);` function is moved to after the retry logic. This ensures that the `name` object is only released after confirming that there will be no retry, thus preventing the use-after-free error when the function attempts to access `name` again during a retry.

